+private LAN); individual endpoints can impose stricter checks.
+**Bypass:** Narrow carve-out for configured paths with appropriate management
+credentials (see [Manage-scope carve-out](#manage-scope-carve-out)).
 
 These routes spawn child processes or execute runtime code. Exposing them to
-non-loopback traffic would allow an attacker who obtained a valid JWT (e.g.,
-via a Cloudflared/Ngrok tunnel) to trigger process spawning — a known CVE
-class ([GHSA-fhh6-4qxv-rpqj](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-fhh6-4qxv-rpqj)).
+remote traffic can allow an attacker who obtained a valid JWT (e.g., via a
+Cloudflared/Ngrok tunnel) to trigger process spawning — a known CVE class
+([GHSA-fhh6-4qxv-rpqj](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-fhh6-4qxv-rpqj)).
 
 **What GHSA-fhh6-4qxv-rpqj is (the attack class):** a management/agent server
 exposes an endpoint that launches a subprocess (`npm install`, `node`, a browser,
 a proxy, `git`, `tar`, …). If that endpoint is reachable from off-host — because
 the operator put OmniRoute behind an nginx/Cloudflare/Tailscale tunnel and a JWT
 leaked, or auth was misconfigured — the attacker turns "call an API" into "run a
-command on the host" (remote code execution). OmniRoute closes this by enforcing a
-**loopback host check unconditionally, before any auth check**, on every
-spawn-capable route: a leaked token over a tunnel still can't reach the spawn.
+command on the host" (remote code execution). The LOCAL_ONLY gate checks trusted
+request locality before the normal authentication branches; remote requests are
+denied unless an explicitly configured carve-out applies.
+
+#### Reverse proxies and locality
+
+The custom server derives locality from the socket peer and its own token-protected
+stamps, not from the HTTP `Host` or client-supplied internal headers. For a
+trusted proxy carrying forwarding headers, a safely attributed loopback or
+private-LAN client is classified as LAN, not loopback; a public client, or a
+missing or invalid client attribution, is remote. A direct request without a
+proxy marker is classified by its socket peer as usual. LOCAL_ONLY permits LAN
+as well as loopback, so LAN access must be considered part of the security
+boundary; restrict network access to OmniRoute and trusted proxies accordingly.
+
+Proxy operators must overwrite, not pass through, client-supplied forwarding
+headers with the actual client address. A loopback proxy that omits forwarding
+headers can make remote callers appear to be direct local callers. Never forge a
+loopback address to bypass a rejection. See the [nginx example](./CORS.md#example-reverse-proxy-in-front-of-omniroute).
+
+The CLI runtime endpoints have an additional strict trusted-loopback requirement:
+a proxied request, including one attributed to a loopback or LAN client, cannot
+use that loopback-only privilege. A management credential does not bypass the
+CLI runtime restriction.
 
